                        "review": "If you enjoyed the first two Life is Strange games, I think The Awesome Adventures of Captain Spirit is worth playing. It's short, but I was surprised by how much I kept thinking about it afterwards.\n\nChris is a nine-year-old boy with a very active imagination. He creates his own superhero persona, Captain Spirit, and turns an ordinary day at home into a little superhero adventure. At first, it feels cute and harmless, but after spending more time with Chris, you start to understand why this imaginary world means so much to him.\n\nChris is dealing with the loss of his mother and everything that's going on at home. His relationship with his father is complicated, and seeing things from Chris's point of view makes some of it hit harder. He's still a child, so he doesn't really understand all the problems around him. Instead, he turns them into games, stories and adventures in his imagination. I thought that was one of the strongest parts of the game.\n\nI especially liked the little puzzles and objectives you come across while exploring the house. They don't feel like they're there just to give you something to do. Exploring the house, completing Captain Spirit's missions and noticing the small details gradually tell you more about Chris and the way he sees the world. The game doesn't spell everything out for you either, which I liked. You have to notice certain things and connect them yourself.\n\nWhat stayed with me most was how differently some scenes feel once you start to understand what Chris is actually going through. It's a small detail, but it makes his imagination feel much more meaningful than it might seem at first.\n\nIt's definitely more of a short episode than a full-length game, but I don't think that's a problem. If you liked the atmosphere of Life is Strange, I think Captain Spirit is worth giving a few hours. For me, it turned out to be a surprisingly touching little story about a kid using his imagination to deal with things that are simply too difficult for him to understand yet.",
